Allow me to tell you how I encountered nethack 5 years ago. While attending
a party, the boyfriend of the hostess wore a shirt featuring a green : and
in small letters around the neck at the back was simply "nethack". Turned
out he was a games programmer in southern ontario (what some Canadians call
"silicon north") and he tells me that he knows programmers who play nethack
to get new ideas for games being developed for console boxes.

He said the game was open source and had been, and is being, developed for
years. He said if you want to try something that is pure gameplay pleasure
with random levels and a chance engine that seems to be able to tell when
you have been beaten down too often and starts pulling you back in. I said
I'd look it up, but I didnt until after my finals. I am glad I did. I played
the same version I play now off and on figuring shizzle out. There are bits
of paper everywhere on my desk with clues as to what "you hear maniacal
laughter in the distance"  and "your skin tingles" might mean. I mean, I had
no idea. But still I played.

I played nethack when I was learning the first version of Diablo, pretty,
but not as compelling. Then about a year ago I started to focus more
seriously on my game. eXplore helped a lot. then I chose to be a monk. I
kept feeling guilty when I ate until I started doing some reading and
spoiled myself. Spoilt myself silly. Well, by my standards anyway. I printed
off all the monk spoilers I found and was astounded at the glut of info I
came across.

So I stopped eXploring and started really learning. But my past monks
(chaotic or neutral) always seemed to die just past sokoban (so the recent
posts about early game were heartening to read) and in eXplore I made it to
some crazy graveyard with demons everywhere and past that point perhaps
twice into some mazes (having lots of money is really handy). I had only
completed the Monk's quest twice and always in eXplore. Once to Fort Ludios.
Once or twice to medusa, but never in a for real scoring game. Then I
discovered the tile sets option. I might go back to the ascii tiles for
nostalgia, but its a joy to see all the creature tiles for the first time
even still and I have been playing the tile set for about a year now.

I couple of months ago I thought I would check the newsgroups and found
rgrn. Now I've done it. Spoilt really good. The newsgroup filled in many of
the blanks and clues I didnt understand or know. But even still, it is with
envy that I see so many yafap.

I doubt that will be Tentee, we still have much to learn, and I obviously
learn slow, but I enjoy it even now as much as I did when I first found it.
More even. To date, I have played all roles/races at least twice, though I
have been trying out wizards for the past few months. Tentee (as in eye dee
ten tee - write out that sequence of letters and numbers) is my 4th attempt
at a Valk. The only conduct I will worry about is my lawful alignment. I
will be surprised if I make it to the end of the week. Expect YAAD soon. But
I remind myself, "with patience and forethought, play each game as if you
will ascend".

I just want to see what the amulet looks like. "Like an amulet, probably" I
remind myself dryly.




